{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789843674,"nonce":"KQ4MQ8VeJQZPLFqqyRJzaf0TgJQ=","title":"The interpreter left at the first call through a pointer and at pow()","message":"Two gaps kept whole call trees out of the interpreter. `HirCallable::\nIndirect` in a `Call` was refused, so `zb_call_1`, the builtin that\ninvokes a Python function value, ran natively, and everything it called\n(`main`, then every function of the kernel) was entered from native\ncode and compiled on the spot by its stub, count or no count. And the\nlibm intrinsics (pow, sin, cos, log, exp) were refused, which took the\nspeed-center harness's own `run_benchmark` out of the interpreter for\nthe same effect.\n\nNow a call through a pointer compiles like `IndirectCall`, and a\npointer the runtime knows as one of the module's functions (a body or\nstub the ground tier published, or code a tier above installed) is\ncalled by id, so an uncompiled target is interpreted. The libm\nintrinsics compute in the interpreter as the native tiers' libm calls\ndo.\n\nStill refused: `CreateUnion` (meteor's `get_puzzle` runs natively).","files":null}]}